{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>שמע ישראל ה׳ וגו׳</b> — Hear, O Israel, HaShem etc. The reason for doubling HaShem twice and not sufficing to say in this manner ״HaShem our Elohim is one״: it intends two levels that HaShem possesses — the first, that He is our Elohim, and the second, that He is one. And the intention in this is that we accept His divinity, blessed be He, upon us for what proceeds from Him, even if it were the case that there were many gods, we would choose none but this Name YHWH — He is our Elohim. And in this are included several particulars: (a) love of the good, love of the beneficial, love of the pleasant. And further, that He is one and there is no other god in the world; and this reason too would suffice to obligate us to subject ourselves to Him and to His commandments, even if it were the case that the reasons for which we chose Him as god did not apply." "<b>Thus we find</b> ourselves saying that by either of the two reasons it would suffice to subject ourselves to His decrees and to His commandments; therefore it said <b>HaShem our Elohim, HaShem is one</b> in two divisions, each one worthy on its own. And if the verse had said ״HaShem our Elohim is one,״ it would have been understood that what they accept upon themselves regarding His divinity is His being one with no other besides Him — which would make the servant obligated to serve his master with no escape from Him, willingly or unwillingly. But it is not so with us and our Elohim, for we desire His divinity out of love of the good, in that He is good in addition to His being one and unified."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:15:35.057033330Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}